import { FilesystemReaderCopy } from "../model"

pub fn copy -> FilesystemReaderCopy = FilesystemReaderCopy {
  summary: "A type holding a checked path spelling, distinct from a string or an open file.",
  taskTitle: "Obtain a Path from text",
  purpose: "Receive reports/report.txt as a report destination. Adding a Path type annotation to text would not validate it. Check the text with paths.parse and pass the successful value to subsequent path operations.",
  setup: "The import makes std/path available under the name paths. paths.parse accepts a String and returns either Left reason or Right file. Left is the failed-check shape and reason is its explanation. Right is the successful shape and file is its Path. match selects the shape and evaluates the expression after ->. Function names and arguments are separated by spaces.",
  after: [
    "This input is valid. paths.render file returns reports/report.txt, and the program prints Saving reports/report.txt. ${...} inside backticks inserts an expression value into the string. The name file is available only within the Right branch.",
    "pub effect fn main is the execution entry point, and println displays a line. For invalid input, show reason turns the error into text after Invalid path:. Reading both branches shows which value can proceed to the next operation.",
    "The opaque declaration means callers do not build the internal representation of a Path directly. Obtain values through library operations such as parse or child. A Path is neither an open file nor proof that a file exists."
  ],
  details: [
    "In let report: paths.Path = \"report.txt\", let gives an immutable value the name report and : paths.Path states its required type. A type annotation does not convert a String, so this definition is rejected with SES-T0101, Binding annotation type mismatch. The repair is to parse the text and use the value carried by Right.",
    "A Path stores a spelling using / as the separator. parse keeps . and .., and render returns that spelling. It does not automatically reconcile case, Unicode representations, or symbolic links.",
    "Even after obtaining a Path, an actual read or write can fail because a file is missing or access is denied. Checking input with Path and reporting execution failures with FileSystemError serve separate purposes."
  ],
  comparisonBefore: "TS file APIs commonly accept a string path. You can add validation before calling them, or distinguish checked values with a dedicated type or wrapper object.",
  comparisonAfter: "Path makes that checked value distinct in the type system. Unlike simply changing a TS type annotation, this example actually examines the input through parse."
}
